<section class="reference-section" id="infinite-shiva-linga"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>The Appearance of the Infinite Shiva Linga</h2><a href="/infinite-shiva-linga.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Shiva Purana · Vidyeshvara Samhita · chapters 5–9.</strong> This original condensed English retelling follows the J. L. Shastri translation linked in the owner-approved source library. The narrative is told from the text’s Shaiva theological perspective; extended praise, ritual claims and graphic details are condensed.</p><div class="source-list"><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc225550.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 5 · The linga’s narrative frame</span><small>Verses 25–30 introduce the manifestation account.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc225551.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 6 · Brahma and Vishnu’s conflict</span><small>The dispute, weapons and the devas’ appeal to Shiva.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc225552.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 7 · The infinite column</span><small>The weapons are absorbed; the searches, Ketaki claim and Shiva’s revelation.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc225553.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 8 · Correction and forgiveness</span><small>Brahma, Vishnu and the Ketaki flower after the revelation.</small></a><a href="https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/book/shiva-purana-english/d/doc225554.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Chapter 9 · Shiva explains the sign</span><small>The column without root or top and the manifest/unmanifest teaching.</small></a></div><ol><li><strong>5.25–30</strong><small>Nandikeshvara introduces the ancient dispute and the unembodied column that removes arrogance.</small></li><li><strong>6.1–19</strong><small>Brahma and Vishnu claim precedence, their attendants fight and destructive weapons meet.</small></li><li><strong>6.20–28; 7.1–12</strong><small>The devas approach Shiva; Shiva reaches the battlefield; the weapons disappear into the column.</small></li><li><strong>7.13–18</strong><small>The decision to search; Vishnu descends as a boar and returns without finding the root.</small></li><li><strong>7.19–28</strong><small>Brahma meets the descending Ketaki flower and returns with a false claim.</small></li><li><strong>7.29–33</strong><small>Shiva emerges; Vishnu acknowledges their error; truthfulness receives favour.</small></li><li><strong>8.1–21</strong><small>The threatened punishment is halted; Brahma’s role continues; Ketaki receives a restricted but not wholly erased place.</small></li><li><strong>9.8–19, 28–43</strong><small>Worship, the column made approachable, pride corrected, and Shiva’s explanation of manifest and unmanifest aspects.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> “Infinite” and “immeasurable” summarize the source’s column without root, middle or top. The page does not identify this one narrative with every teaching about the twelve Jyotirlingas or every Shaiva explanation of the linga. The reflection on admitting one’s limit is editorial.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. The hero preserves Brahma on the hamsa and four-armed Vishnu on Garuda before the unbounded column. The inline image keeps the correction non-graphic and shows Shiva with Uma, following the chapter’s setting. The square card is symbolic. No image is reused elsewhere.</p></div></section>
